  "output_text": "Introduction/Context\n- Study design and dataset: In a prospective endometrial carcinoma (EC) cohort, protein-level differential analysis was performed comparing hotspot-mutated CTNNB1 tumors to WT CTNNB1 tumors within the CNV-L subgroup. The uploaded CSV (NIHMS1921121-supplement-7-CTNNB1HotspotvsWT-Protein.csv) provides per-protein results (L2FC, T-Score, P-Val, FDR) for n around 10k–11k features; CTNNB1 itself is among the upregulated proteins (L2FC = 0.76; T-Score = 10.29; P-Val = 3.39e-15; FDR = 1.72e-11). Several components of the Wnt/CTNNB1 axis and related pathways are enriched among the top hits, including AXIN1 (L2FC = 0.67), DKK4 (L2FC = 4.05), and LEF1 (L2FC = 1.31). The dataset also contains notable upregulations of other Wnt-associated genes (e.g., WIF1, LRP4) and downregulations of a subset of non-Wnt pathway genes (e.g., HCLS1 with L2FC = −0.92). In a separate analysis described in the context, CTNNB1 S45 phosphorylation was significantly downregulated in hotspot-mutated tumors, while phosphosites distant from hotspot mutations tended to be upregulated. The current results are anchored to the mutation state of CTNNB1 (hotspot-mutated vs WT) and to the CNV-L context.\n- Key interpretation point: The positive CTNNB1 protein signal in hotspot-mutant tumors, together with upregulation of LEF1 and Wnt pathway modulators (DKK4, WIF1, LRP4), supports activation of canonical CTNNB1 signaling in this mutation state. The reciprocal downregulation of CTNNB1 S45 phosphorylation found in the separate analysis aligns with stabilization of CTNNB1 protein, facilitating downstream transcriptional programs.\n\nKeywords\n- CTNNB1, hotspot mutation, WT CTNNB1, endometrial carcinoma, CNV-L, protein expression, L2FC, S45 phosphorylation, Wnt signaling, LEF1, AXIN1, DKK4, WIF1, LRP4\n\nCandidate Genes\n- CTNNB1\n- LEF1\n- AXIN1\n- DKK4\n- WIF1\n- LRP4\n\nTargeted (keyword-guided) subset\n- Upregulated in hotspot CTNNB1 vs WT with relevance to the Wnt/CTNNB1 axis: CTNNB1, LEF1, AXIN1, DKK4, WIF1, LRP4\n- Downregulated subset with potential non-Wnt context: HCLS1, KIAA0513, LSP1, ARHGAP9, ARHGAP45, DOCK8, ARHGAP4\n- Focused interpretation: The Up group comprises canonical Wnt signaling components and regulators whose coordinated elevation is consistent with enhanced CTNNB1 pathway activity in hotspot-mutant tumors. The Down group includes genes with negative fold changes that may reflect cell-type– or context-specific modules that are not directly part of canonical Wnt signaling in this dataset.\n\nKey Genes/Proteins (leading edge)\nUp (Leading edge)\n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |\n| CTNNB1 | Up | Beta-catenin; central effector of canonical Wnt signaling; transactivates target genes when stabilized | Wnt/CTNNB1 |\n| LEF1 | Up | Transcription factor partnering with CTNNB1 to drive Wnt target genes | Wnt/CTNNB1 |\n| AXIN1 | Up | Component of the β-catenin destruction complex; negative regulator that can participate in feedback | Wnt/CTNNB1 |\n| DKK4 | Up | Secreted Wnt pathway modulator (inhibitor) with context-specific roles; part of feedback regulation | Wnt signaling |\n| WIF1 | Up | Wnt inhibitory factor; secreted antagonist of Wnt signaling | Wnt signaling |\n| LRP4 | Up | Co-receptor/ modulator in Wnt signaling context; contributes to receptor complex dynamics | Wnt signaling |\n\nDown (Leading edge)\n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |\n| HCLS1 | Down | Hematopoietic cell-specific Lyn substrate-1; cytoskeletal signaling; not a canonical Wnt component in this context | Cytoskeletal/Immune signaling |\n| KIAA0513 | Down | Not well characterized in this dataset; potential non-Wnt modulatory roles | Not specified |\n| LSP1 | Down | Lymphocyte-specific protein 1; cytoskeletal regulator | Cytoskeletal signaling |\n| ARHGAP9 | Down | RhoGTPase-activating protein; cytoskeletal remodeling | Cytoskeletal signaling |\n| ARHGAP45 | Down | RhoGAP family member; cytoskeletal regulation | Cytoskeletal signaling |\n| DOCK8 | Down | DOCK family member; cytoskeletal reorganization; immune signaling | Cytoskeletal/ signaling |\n\nComparisons\n- Mutation state vs tissue context: The observed upregulation of CTNNB1 and several canonical Wnt targets (LEF1) in hotspot-mutant CTNNB1 tumors is consistent with the famed effect of stabilizing CTNNB1 and promoting transcription of Wnt targets. The concurrent upregulation of DKK4 and WIF1 suggests the presence of feedback inhibitors attempting to restrain signaling, which is a common feature in dynamic Wnt signaling states. The presence of AXIN1 among the upregulated hits further supports engagement of the destruction complex components in a feedback-augmented state. The Down group largely contains genes not classically tied to the Wnt axis in EC or cytoskeletal regulators, hinting at broader tumor-context effects or CNV-L–specific perturbations. No single-modality discordance is apparent within the provided protein panel; however, phosphorylation data (S45) from the separate analysis aligns mechanistically with CTNNB1 stabilization and downstream activation.\n- Cross-modality note: The phosphorylation data (S45 down in hotspot mutants) provides a complementary mechanistic link to the protein-level upregulation of CTNNB1 and target genes, supporting a coherent model of stabilization and transcriptional activation in hotspot CTNNB1 tumors.\n\nImplications\n- Therapeutic relevance: The pattern of CTNNB1 upregulation together with LEF1 activation and feedback inhibitors (DKK4, WIF1) supports reliance on canonical Wnt signaling in hotspot CTNNB1 EC tumors. This may influence sensitivity to agents targeting Wnt/CTNNB1 signaling; CTNNB1 stabilization and downstream transcriptional output could underpin tumor proliferation or survival in this context.\n- Resistance considerations: The simultaneous upregulation of Wnt inhibitors (DKK4, WIF1) may reflect intrinsic feedback attempting to restrain signaling, potentially impacting the efficacy or kinetics of Wnt-targeted therapies.\n\nNext Steps & Clarification\n- Validation of phosphorylation-state context: Integrate the CTNNB1 S45 phosphorylation data with the current protein panel by parallel phosphoproteomics or site-specific targeted assays to confirm the mechanistic link between decreased S45 phosphorylation and the observed protein-upregulation pattern.\n- Nuclear localization and transcriptional activity: Assess CTNNB1 subcellular localization (cytoplasmic vs nuclear) and direct transcriptional readouts (e.g., CTNNB1/LEF1 target gene expression) in hotspot-mutant vs WT tumors to confirm functional activation.\n- Broader panel validation: Expand validation to additional EC samples or independent cohorts to confirm the robustness of DKK4/WIF1 upregulation and AXIN1 elevation as consistent features of hotspot CTNNB1 tumors within CNV-L.\n- Functional interrogation: In vitro or in vivo models bearing CTNNB1 hotspot mutations could be used to test whether perturbation of LEF1 or DKK4/WIF1 modifies tumor growth, providing a functional readout for potential combination strategies with Wnt pathway inhibitors.\n\nReferences\n- PMIDs were not provided in the current outputs; no reference list is included here. If PMIDs from validated steps become available, they can be appended in a References section.",
